---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import OpenGraphGenerator from '../../components/OpenGraphGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Open Graph Tag Generator" description="Generate Open Graph and Twitter Card meta tags so your links look right when shared on social media.">
  <OpenGraphGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why unshared-looking links hurt click-through</h2>
  <p>A link shared without proper Open Graph tags shows up as bare text or a generic placeholder — no image, no title, no context — which measurably reduces how often people click compared to a rich preview card with a compelling image and description.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your page's title, description, and preview image URL.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated Open Graph and Twitter Card meta tags into your page's head.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Open Graph Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using a relative image URL</strong> instead of an absolute one — social platforms fetching your page need a full URL to load the preview image correctly.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting Twitter Card tags separately</strong> — while Twitter/X falls back to Open Graph tags in many cases, dedicated Twitter Card tags give more reliable control over that platform's preview.</li>
    <li><strong>Not testing after publishing</strong> — social platforms often cache preview data, so use each platform's debugging/preview tool to verify and refresh the cache after changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Open Graph Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Making a blog post or product page look good when shared on social media</li>
    <li>Ensuring a consistent preview image across different sharing platforms</li>
    <li>Debugging why a shared link isn't showing a preview image</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Open Graph Tags FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Why isn't my new image showing up when I share the link?</strong> Most platforms cache Open Graph data — use the platform's specific debugging tool (like Facebook's Sharing Debugger) to force a re-scrape of the updated tags.</p>
</ToolLayout>
